Yes. That’s one of the classic strategies in behavioral genetics, because it lets you vary one source of similarity while holding another relatively constant.

A simplified view is:

Comparison	Genetics	Environment	What it helps estimate
Identical twins raised together	Very similar	Very similar	Baseline similarity
Fraternal twins raised together	Less similar	Very similar	Added effect of genetics
Ordinary siblings	Less similar	Similar (often less than twins)	Combined family + genetics
Identical twins raised apart	Very similar	Different	Environmental effects on shared genetics
Adoptive siblings	Unrelated	Similar	Environmental effects with minimal shared genetics

The logic is essentially the triangulation idea you’ve been talking about.

Instead of locating a point in physical space, you’re locating the contribution of different causes.

Observed Trait
      ▲
     /|\
    / | \
Genes Environment Chance

Each comparison gives you another “anchor.” No single comparison tells the whole story, but multiple independent comparisons constrain the range of plausible explanations.

That mirrors your broader Pantheon idea:

Unknown Cause
      ▲
     /|\
 Dataset A
 Dataset B
 Dataset C

Each new perspective reduces ambiguity.

One thing modern research has also emphasized is that it’s rarely just “biology vs. environment.” Genes and environment interact. For example:

* A genetic predisposition might only express itself in certain environments.
* The same environment can affect different people differently depending on their genetics.
* People also influence their environments—for example, personality traits can lead someone to seek out particular experiences.

So a richer model is:

Outcome =
    Genetics
  + Environment
  + Gene × Environment interaction
  + Developmental history
  + Random biological variation

That interaction term is especially interesting because it resembles your recurring idea that meaning often emerges from relationships rather than isolated components. The behavior isn’t fully “in the genes” or “in the environment”—it’s partly in how they combine under specific conditions. That relational perspective has become increasingly important in genetics, neuroscience, and developmental psychology.